 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Out-of-bounds inputs:** Coordinates outside $[0, 1]$ cause undefined behavior if not clamped. The implementation explicitly checks bounds and zeros outputs/grads instead of clamping [S12, S15, unofficial].
-*   **Hash collisions:** Collisions occur when different integer grid coordinates hash to the same table index. The method relies on the neural network and gradient-based optimization to disambiguate these collisions without explicit handling [S16]. At fine resolutions, colliding training gradients average, meaning the largest gradients dominate, automatically prioritizing sparse areas with important fine-scale detail [S4, unofficial]. However, if the table size is too small, distant point collisions can introduce artifacts [S9].
-*   **Precision and Performance:** Half-precision `atomicAdd` is slow for scalar features ($F=1$) on older architectures; vectorized `__half2` operations are used when $C$ is even [S12, S15, unofficial]. Performance drops significantly if $2 \cdot T \cdot F > 6 \cdot 2^{20}$ (6 MB L2 cache limit on RTX 3090) [S4, unofficial]. Skipping Adam steps for zero gradients saves $\sim 10\%$ performance when gradients are sparse [S4, unofficial].
